stm32f7: stop terminal I2C interrupt tail-chaining

The transfer-complete condition is level-sensitive and is cleared by the
peripheral only after STOP progresses.  The terminal ISR currently wakes the
waiting task while leaving TCIE enabled, relying on that task to mask the I2C
interrupts.  If TC remains asserted across exception return, Cortex-M
tail-chains back into the event ISR indefinitely and the waiter never runs.

Mask the transfer interrupt sources once the ISR has reached its terminal
state, before posting the waiter.  The waiting path already performs the same
mask after it resumes, so this merely closes the unsafe scheduling gap.

--- a/arch/arm/src/stm32f7/stm32_i2c.c
+++ b/arch/arm/src/stm32f7/stm32_i2c.c
@@ -2103,5 +2103,14 @@ static int stm32_i2c_isr_process(struct stm32_i2c_priv_s *priv)
 #else
 
+      /* TC remains level-sensitive until STOP progresses.  Do not leave the
+       * event source enabled while waking the waiter: if TC is still asserted
+       * on exception return, Cortex-M tail-chains back here and the task that
+       * normally masks these sources never gets CPU time.
+       */
+
+      stm32_i2c_modifyreg32(priv, STM32_I2C_CR1_OFFSET,
+                            I2C_CR1_ALLINTS, 0);
+
       /* We will update private state to capture NACK which is used in
        * combination with the astart flag to report the type of NACK received
        * (address vs data) to the upper layers once we exit the ISR.
